<section class="sub-hero"><div class="sub-hero-ph" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/assets/img/photos/consultation.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/consultation-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/consultation.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.18"></div><div class="wrap sub-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-solutions/">Solutions</a></li><li aria-current="page">Appraisal Clause Representation</li></ol></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal solution</p><h1>Minnesota Appraisal Clause Representation</h1><p class="lede">Your insurer accepts the claim but its number is far below what the repairs will cost. The appraisal provision gives you a way to resolve that gap without a lawsuit, and we can be the appraiser you appoint.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-sky" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="i" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Our role</dt><dd>Policyholder’s appraiser</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Decides</dt><dd>Amount of loss</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>Where the provision comes from</h2>
<p>Minnesota is unusual in that the appraisal process is not just a contract term an insurer chose to include. The state’s standard fire policy statute, Minn. Stat. § 65A.01, contains an appraisal provision that property policies written here generally follow. In plain terms it says that when the insured and the company disagree about the amount of loss, either may demand appraisal in writing. Each side names a “competent and disinterested” appraiser, the two appraisers select an umpire, and a written award signed by any two of the three sets the amount.</p>
<p>The wording in your own policy is what controls, so the first thing we do is read it. Some forms add deadlines for naming an appraiser, cost-sharing language for the umpire, or rules about how replacement cost and actual cash value are reported on the award.</p>
<h2>What the panel can and cannot decide</h2>
<p>Appraisal answers a narrow question: how much is the loss worth? It does not decide whether the policy covers the loss at all. In <strong>Quade v. Secura Insurance</strong> (2012), the Minnesota Supreme Court addressed a related point and held that appraisers may determine the cause of the damage when doing so is part of measuring the amount of loss, while questions of coverage remain for the courts. That decision is frequently cited in Minnesota appraisal clause disputes, but how it applies to any one claim is a legal question. If coverage itself is in dispute, talk with an attorney.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Plain limits</b>We are appraisers. We are not attorneys and we are not public adjusters. We do not give legal advice, interpret coverage for you, or negotiate the claim outside the appraisal process.</div>
<h2>How we work once we are named</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Policy and file review.</strong> We gather the declarations page, the full policy, the insurer’s estimate, photos, correspondence and any contractor bids you already have.</li>
<li><strong>Our own inspection.</strong> We walk the property, take measurements and document the damage independently rather than marking up someone else’s scope.</li>
<li><strong>A defensible valuation.</strong> We build a line-item estimate priced for your part of Minnesota, whether that is the Twin Cities metro, Rochester, St. Cloud or a lake cabin up north.</li>
<li><strong>Working with the other appraiser.</strong> We compare scopes item by item. Many differences are resolved here, which keeps the umpire’s role small.</li>
<li><strong>Umpire selection and presentation.</strong> If the two appraisers cannot agree on an umpire, the policy or statute usually allows either side to ask a court to appoint one. Remaining items are then presented with photos and pricing support.</li>
<li><strong>Award review.</strong> Before anyone signs, we make sure the award clearly states the figures your policy needs, such as replacement cost and actual cash value.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Who can send the demand</h2>
<p>Either party can invoke appraisal. Sometimes the insurer sends the demand, which surprises homeowners who assumed it was only a policyholder tool. Whoever starts it, the other side generally must participate and name its own appraiser. A demand is usually most effective when the disagreement is clearly about price and scope rather than about whether the loss is covered at all, and when you can point to a specific figure you believe is correct, such as a detailed contractor bid.</p>
<h2>Timing you should keep in mind</h2>
<p>Minnesota’s standard policy language commonly requires that any lawsuit be brought within two years after the loss. Appraisal does not automatically pause that clock. Confirm the limit in your own policy and with an attorney early, especially if your claim has already been open for many months.</p>
<p>Minnesota also has a statute, Minn. Stat. § 604.18, that lets a court award certain amounts when an insurer denies benefits without a reasonable basis. It has its own procedural requirements and is a matter for an attorney, not something an appraiser invokes.</p>
<h2>Starting the conversation</h2>
<p>If you have received an appraisal demand, or you are thinking about sending one, Minnesota Insurance Appraiser can review the paperwork and tell you plainly whether the Minnesota appraisal clause looks like a sensible path. Read the <a href="/appraisal-process-guide/">appraisal process guide</a> for the full sequence, or <a href="/contact-us/">contact us</a> to talk through your file.</p>

<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can I pick anyone as my appraiser?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most Minnesota policies require the appraiser to be competent and disinterested, which generally means someone qualified who does not have a financial stake in the outcome. Your contractor or a relative may be challenged on that basis.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does invoking appraisal mean I am suing my insurer?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. Appraisal is a contractual process for setting the amount of loss. It is separate from litigation, although some disputes involve both.</p></div></details><details><summary>Who pays the umpire?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Many policies split the umpire’s fee equally while each side pays its own appraiser. Check the appraisal paragraph in your policy for the exact wording.</p></div></details></div>
